\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 1. Introduction and Motivation\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 Postpartum Depression (PPD) is a common but under-diagnosed mental health condition affecting mothers after childbirth. In Bangladesh, screening relies primarily on clinical instruments such as the Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale (EPDS) and the Patient Health Questionnaire-9 (PHQ-9), both of which require a trained interviewer and are not applied universally. A machine learning model that can flag high-risk mothers using only routinely-collectable demographic, economic, family, and pregnancy-history information -- without requiring the EPDS/PHQ-9 interview itself -- could support earlier, more scalable screening.\par

An existing external study on a similar Bangladesh dataset reports near-perfect discrimination (AUROC), which is a strong indicator of data leakage: the model was very likely trained using answers to the EPDS/PHQ-9 items themselves (or scores derived from them) as predictors of a label that is directly computed from those same items. Such a model cannot be deployed in practice, because at prediction time a mother who has not yet completed an EPDS/PHQ-9 interview has none of those answers available.\par

This project builds a machine learning framework that is explicitly designed to avoid this failure mode, while introducing a genuine methodological contribution -- four engineered Composite Risk Indices -- and a thorough, multi-angle explainability analysis so that the model's predictions can be trusted and clinically interpreted rather than treated as a black box.\par
\page

\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 2. Dataset Description\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 The dataset is the Mendeley "Data for Postpartum Depression Prediction in Bangladesh" dataset: 800 postpartum mothers, 70 raw columns (69 after removing a redundant index column). It contains demographic, economic, family/social, pregnancy, delivery, neonatal, and prior mental-health-history questions, plus the 10 EPDS items and 9 PHQ-9 items used to compute two clinical scores and their corresponding severity categories.\par

\pard\f1\fs22\b\sa60 Column roles\b0\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 - Target candidates: EPDS Score, EPDS Result (Low/Medium/High), PHQ9 Score, PHQ9 Result (Minimal/Mild/Moderate/Moderately Severe/Severe), and an engineered binary target PPD_binary (EPDS Score >= 13 -> 1, following the convention used in the reference literature).\par
- Leakage columns (23 total, never used as model input): the 10 EPDS items, the 9 PHQ-9 items, and the 4 score/category columns they produce.\par
- Safe input features (46 raw columns): demographic, family, economic, pregnancy, delivery, neonatal, and prior (pre-postpartum) mental-health-history questions -- none of which are derived from the EPDS/PHQ-9 interview itself.\par

\pard\f1\fs22\b\sa60 Target class distributions (n = 800)\b0\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 EPDS Result: High 349, Low 247, Medium 204. PHQ9 Result: Mild 263, Moderate 237, Moderately Severe 132, Severe 89, Minimal 79. PPD_binary: Positive 349, Negative 451. All three targets were recomputed directly from the numeric EPDS/PHQ-9 scores using the standard clinical cut-offs (rather than trusting the dataset's original category labels), which corrected 16 mislabeled EPDS Result rows and 40 mislabeled PHQ9 Result rows -- including every row that had been labeled with the non-standard category "Normal", which does not exist in the official PHQ-9 severity scale.\par
\page

\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 3. Overall Methodology and Workflow\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 The project follows a strict, auditable pipeline in which every stage's output feeds the next, and every script is independently re-runnable and logged:\par
\pard\f1\fs20\f2 Raw CSV (n=800, 70 cols)\par
   ->01_dataset_overview.py  -- classify every column as TARGET / LEAKAGE / SAFE INPUT\par
   ->02_data_cleaning.py  -- fix label typos, fill structural missing values, mode-impute true-random missing values, recompute targets from scores\par
   ->03_association_eda.py  -- chi-square / ANOVA association of every raw feature vs. every target\par
   ->04_feature_engineering.py  -- build the 4 Composite Risk Indices (equal-weight and chi-square-weighted variants)\par
   ->05_direction_check.py  -- empirically verify every ordinal sub-feature mapping's direction against real PPD rates\par
   ->06_encoding.py  -- one-hot encode categoricals, drop leakage columns, produce the final model-ready table\par
   ->07 / 07b_modeling.py  -- baseline models, then hyperparameter tuning + deep learning + stacking\par
   ->08_ablation_study.py  -- Raw-only vs. Raw+Composite vs. Selected+Composite, with paired significance testing\par
   ->09_shap_explainability.py  -- SHAP-based global and local explanations of the chosen model\par
   ->10-20  -- ordinal-aware evaluation, threshold/calibration tuning, CatBoost native handling, Tier-2 refinements, literature grounding, and five further explainability/discovery experiments (SHAP interactions, clustering, error analysis, closing-loop feature test)\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 At no point after script 06 do the raw EPDS/PHQ-9 item answers or scores re-enter the feature set -- this constraint is enforced in code (an explicit drop list), not just by convention, in every one of the 20 scripts.\par
\page

\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 4. Novel Contribution: Composite Risk Indices\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 The central methodological contribution of this project is to replace a flat bag of 108 raw one-hot survey answers with four clinically-grounded, interpretable composite scores, each summarizing a group of related raw questions into a single 0-10 index.\par

\pard\f1\fs22\b\sa60 General formula\b0\par
\pard\f1\fs20\f2\sa120 normalize(x) = (score(x) - min) / (max - min)\par
Composite Index = 10 \u215 \u931 ( w_i \u215 normalize(sub-feature_i) )\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 Two weighting schemes for w_i were built and compared: \b Equal-weight\b0  (w_i = 1/n, every sub-feature counts the same) and \b Chi-square-weighted\b0  (w_i is proportional to -log10(p_i), where p_i is that sub-feature's own chi-square association p-value against PPD_binary, so a sub-feature that is individually a stronger risk marker counts for more inside the composite). The weighted variant was adopted as final: under equal-weighting, the Maternal Mental-Health Risk Index became statistically NON-significant because its strongest sub-feature (Abuse) was diluted by weaker/noisier co-features -- a concrete, empirical justification for the weighting choice rather than an arbitrary preference.\par

\pard\f1\fs22\b\sa60 The four indices\b0\par
\trowd\trgaph80\trleft0\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10
\cellx1900\cellx5100\cellx9360
\pard\intbl\f1\fs19\b Index\cell Sub-features used\cell Interpretation\cell\b0\row
\trowd\trgaph80\trleft0\cellx1900\cellx5100\cellx9360
\pard\intbl\f1\fs19 Economic Stability Index (ESI)\cell Current monthly income, Husband's monthly income, Occupation after childbirth, Education Level\cell Higher = more economically stable / protective\cell\row
\trowd\trgaph80\trleft0\cellx1900\cellx5100\cellx9360
