\pard\f1\fs22\sa120\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 All 4 weighted composite indices are statistically significant (both ANOVA and Kruskal-Wallis, p < 0.05) against all 3 targets -- 24 out of 24 tests -- confirmed in \f2 composite_index_association.csv\f1 . Two notable, empirically-corrected direction findings (verified in script 05 against real PPD rates, not assumed): (1) more education was associated with a HIGHER observed PPD rate in this sample (University 48.5% vs. Primary 22.2%), plausibly reflecting greater symptom awareness/reporting, so the Education sub-score was reverse-mapped to keep ESI's "higher = protective" meaning internally consistent; (2) the raw "Abuse" question's "No" answers were associated with a markedly HIGHER PPD rate than "Yes" answers (64.1% vs. 27.0%), the opposite of the naive clinical assumption -- the composite mapping was aligned to this empirical pattern, and this direction was independently reproduced by two later, unrelated methods (a Logistic-Regression-learned weighting scheme in Step 13, and the raw SHAP feature ranking in Step 9), which rules out the possibility that it is an artifact of the original chi-square weighting choice specifically. This finding is flagged in the Limitations section as needing verification of the original Bangla questionnaire wording.\par
\page

\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 5. Data Cleaning and Leakage-Free Design\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 Cleaning addressed four distinct classes of data-quality issues, each handled with a different, documented strategy rather than one blanket approach:\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa60 (a) Category-label inconsistencies (typos, case mismatches, stray Bangla text in Likert-scale items) were unified via explicit mapping dictionaries.\par
(b) Structural missing values (e.g. no addiction, no pregnancy loss, only one child) were filled with an explicit, honest category such as "No Addiction" or "Not Applicable" -- never with a statistical average, since the value is not randomly missing, it does not apply.\par
(c) True random missing values (Abuse: 38, Husband's monthly income: 28, Husband's education: 9, Education Level: 6, Trust and share feelings: 1) were mode-imputed, each paired with a "_was_missing" flag column so the model can still learn from the fact that a value was originally missing.\par
(d) The EPDS Result and PHQ9 Result category labels were recomputed directly from the numeric scores using the standard clinical cut-offs (EPDS: 0-8 Low, 9-12 Medium, 13-30 High; PHQ-9: 0-4 Minimal, 5-9 Mild, 10-14 Moderate, 15-19 Moderately Severe, 20-27 Severe), fixing 16 + 40 internally-inconsistent rows respectively.\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 The leakage-free design is enforced at the encoding stage (script 06): the 10 EPDS items, 9 PHQ-9 items, and both raw scores are permanently dropped from the feature table before any model ever sees the data, and the three targets are carried alongside the features but never included as an input to each other's model.\par
\page

\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 6. Baseline Modeling (Step 7)\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 Five model families (Logistic Regression, Random Forest, XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost) were trained per target on the full raw+composite feature set, using stratified 5-fold cross-validation plus an 80/20 holdout split. See Section 17 for the full comparison table. Random Forest was the strongest baseline for EPDS Result (CV F1-macro 0.583) and a close second for the other two targets; results for PHQ9 Result in particular showed low absolute F1-macro (~0.34-0.36) across every baseline model, an early signal that this 5-class ordinal target would need special handling (addressed later in Step 10).\par
\page

\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 7. Advanced Modeling: Tuning, Deep Learning, and Stacking (Step 7b)\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 Each of the four tree-based baselines was tuned via RandomizedSearchCV, an MLP (small feed-forward neural network) architecture search was run, and a Stacking Ensemble (all four tuned tree models feeding a Logistic Regression meta-learner) was tested. Full hyperparameters selected per target are recorded in \f2 best_hyperparameters.json\f1 . Key result: for PPD_binary, the Stacking Ensemble achieved the single best result found anywhere in the project (CV F1-macro 0.762, holdout F1-macro 0.765) -- better than any single baseline or single tuned model. For EPDS Result, tuned Random Forest achieved the best CV F1-macro (0.590) but a WORSE holdout F1-macro (0.502) than the untuned baseline (0.520/0.540 depending on split), a sign of mild overfitting to the CV folds during hyperparameter search on a modest n=800 dataset -- discussed further in Section 17.\par
\page

\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 8. Ablation Study: Proving the Composite Indices Add Value (Step 8)\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 This is the core evidence for the project's central novelty claim. Three feature "arms" were compared under an identical modeling protocol, for every model family and every target:\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa60 Arm A "Raw only" (108 features) -- no composite indices.\par
Arm B "Raw + Composite" (112 features) -- Arm A plus the 4 chi-square-weighted composite indices.\par
Arm C "Selected + Composite" (30 features) -- the top-30 most important features from Arm B, selected by Random Forest importance, re-derived independently inside every cross-validation fold's training portion (a NESTED selection procedure, fixed after an earlier version selected features once on the full dataset -- a subtle form of data leakage that was caught and corrected).\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 Result (averaged across all 5 models): composite features helped for EPDS Result and PHQ9 Result, and were roughly neutral for PPD_binary, in the original 5-fold test. Because a simple mean comparison does not establish whether a difference is real or noise, a paired t-test and Wilcoxon signed-rank test were added on the same 5 folds -- the original test found 0 of 30 arm-vs-arm comparisons statistically significant at p<0.05, a low-power result given only 5 paired observations. Re-running the same comparison with RepeatedStratifiedKFold (5 folds \u215 5 repeats = 25 paired observations) found 2 of 15 comparisons significant (EPDS Result \u215 XGBoost, +0.022, p=0.0008; PHQ9 Result \u215 LightGBM, +0.022, p=0.002), confirming that the original null result reflected low statistical power rather than an absent effect -- and every other comparison remained positive in direction even where short of significance.\par
\page

\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 9. Explainable AI: SHAP Analysis (Step 9)\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 The explainability backbone model is a Random Forest (n_estimators=300, class_weight="balanced") trained on Arm B's 112 features, explained via \f2 shap.TreeExplainer\f1  on the held-out 20% test set. Random Forest was deliberately chosen over the Stacking Ensemble (Step 7b's best raw performer for PPD_binary) because TreeExplainer gives fast, exact Shapley values for a single tree ensemble, while a 4-model stacking ensemble would require a slower, approximate explanation method -- a conscious clarity-over-marginal-accuracy trade-off, consistent with the project's stated goal of being \i explainable\i0 , not merely accurate.\par

