\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 The composite indices dominate the global top ranking: 4/4 in the top-10 for EPDS Result and PPD_binary, 3/4 for PHQ9 Result -- and for PHQ9 Result specifically, the Social Support Index is the single MOST important feature overall, ahead of every raw answer. Train accuracy was 1.000 for all three targets (normal, expected behavior for an unconstrained Random Forest on a modest dataset, not a leakage symptom, since held-out test accuracy is realistic and matches the CV numbers). Concrete patient-level examples: for EPDS Result, the model's predicted probability of the "High" risk class ranged from 0.933 for its most confident high-risk example down to 0.037 for its most confident low-risk example -- a wide, well-separated range. For PHQ9 Result, even the model's most confident "Severe" prediction only reached P=0.483, a concrete, patient-level confirmation that PHQ9's five ordinal bands blur into each other and are the hardest target to separate cleanly -- directly motivating the ordinal-aware methods introduced in Step 10.\par
\page

\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 10. Ordinal-Aware Modeling: QWK and Regression-Threshold (Step 10)\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 EPDS Result and PHQ9 Result are ORDINAL targets (Low < Medium < High; Minimal < ... < Severe), but a plain multi-class classifier and plain F1-macro treat every misclassification as equally wrong -- "predicted Low, actual High" counts the same as "predicted Moderate, actual Moderately Severe." Quadratic Weighted Kappa (QWK) is the standard ordinal-aware metric, penalizing distant misses more than adjacent ones, and was added here for the first time. In parallel, a REGRESSION-THEN-THRESHOLD approach was tested: predict the continuous EPDS/PHQ9 Score directly (5 regressor families), then apply the exact same clinical cut-offs used to build the official labels, and compare this against the direct classifier on an identical split.\par

\pard\f1\fs22\b\sa60 Findings\b0\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 QWK was notably higher than F1-macro for both targets (EPDS: 0.540 -> 0.557; PHQ9: 0.329 -> 0.548 for the direct classifier), confirming most model errors are near-miss/adjacent-band rather than wild misses -- both targets are more clinically useful than F1-macro alone suggested. For PHQ9 Result, Regression + Threshold (Linear Regression) genuinely beat the direct classifier on every metric: accuracy 0.344 -> 0.463, F1-macro 0.329 -> 0.426, QWK 0.548 -> 0.602 -- adopted as the final PHQ9 Result approach. For EPDS Result, the direct classifier remained best (QWK 0.557 vs. best regression 0.521) -- reported honestly as no universal winner across targets. A caveat found along the way: Random Forest regression compresses predictions toward the mean (a classic ensemble-averaging effect), which badly hurt its categorical conversion for PHQ9 (F1-macro only 0.251 despite R\up6 2\up0\nosupersub  = 0.433) -- Linear Regression or CatBoost are the better regressor choices for this specific thresholding use case.\par
\page

\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 11. Decision Threshold Tuning and Probability Calibration (Step 11)\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 For PPD_binary, the default 0.5 decision threshold is an arbitrary convention, not necessarily optimal. Precision-recall-curve-based threshold tuning found a max-F1 threshold of 0.407, which gave the same accuracy (0.775) but better F1 (0.731 -> 0.753) and notably better recall (0.700 -> 0.786) at only a small precision cost -- adopted as the new default operating point. A "recall >= 85% screening" threshold of 0.303 was also documented as an alternative for a high-recall screening use case (accuracy drops to 0.6875, precision to 0.600, but recall reaches 0.857) -- both options are reported so the deployment context (cost of a missed case vs. a false alarm) can decide which to use. Separately, CalibratedClassifierCV (Platt/sigmoid scaling) gave a small, expected improvement in probability calibration (Brier score 0.1677 -> 0.1667) with ROC-AUC essentially unchanged (0.829 -> 0.828), confirming calibration reshaped the probability values without hurting discrimination -- the calibrated probabilities are recommended whenever a patient-facing risk score (not just a binary flag) needs to be reported.\par
\page

\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 12. CatBoost Native Categorical Handling and Repeated CV (Step 12)\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 Part A compared CatBoost's native categorical-feature handling (no one-hot encoding, 59 features) against the standard one-hot pipeline (116 features) on an identical split, for all three targets. Result: no universal winner -- native categorical won clearly on PHQ9 Result's holdout split (F1-macro 0.400 vs. 0.336) and was roughly tied elsewhere, but achieved comparable results using barely half the features, a genuine simplicity/efficiency finding worth reporting even without a raw accuracy win. Part B re-ran the Step 8 ablation significance test using RepeatedStratifiedKFold (5 folds \u215 5 repeats = 25 paired observations instead of 5) for higher statistical power, described in Section 8 above.\par
\page

\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 13. Second-Round Improvement Experiments (Tier 2)\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 Three further, independent improvement ideas were tested and evaluated on their own merits -- two were rejected after honest evaluation, illustrating methodological rigor rather than only reporting wins.\par

\pard\f1\fs22\b\sa60 (a) Data-driven composite weight learning\b0\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 Per-domain Logistic Regression was used to learn each composite's sub-feature weights directly from the data (using the LR's own predicted probability, scaled, as the new composite score), compared against the original chi-square-weighted composites. Both variants were 12/12 significant on association tests (a tie); predictively, the LR-learned version won for EPDS Result (0.590 vs. 0.578) and PHQ9 Result (0.371 vs. 0.365), while chi-square-weighting won for PPD_binary (0.753 vs. 0.746) -- no universal winner, so the simpler, already-validated chi-square weighting was kept as final. A notable bonus finding: the independently-learned LR coefficients reproduced the same counter-intuitive "Abuse" direction finding described in Section 4, a third independent confirmation that this is a genuine data pattern rather than an artifact of one specific weighting method.\par

\pard\f1\fs22\b\sa60 (b) SMOTENC oversampling and MICE-style imputation\b0\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 SMOTENC (fold-safe, applied only to each fold's training portion) gave a tiny improvement for EPDS Result (0.578 -> 0.583) and PPD_binary (0.753 -> 0.758), but made PHQ9 Result WORSE (0.365 -> 0.348) -- exactly the target it was meant to help most, since it has the most severe class imbalance. The likely cause: with ~112 mostly-binary one-hot features, "nearest neighbor" becomes unreliable in high-dimensional space (the curse of dimensionality), so SMOTE's synthetic minority patients end up as unrealistic combinations of survey answers. Decision: not adopted; class_weight="balanced" alone remains the right approach. Separately, IterativeImputer (MICE-style) was compared against the original mode-fill for 5 true-random-missing columns: it agreed completely for binary columns with a dominant category, but disagreed substantially for multi-category columns (e.g. Husband's monthly income: 28/28 values changed) -- yet downstream PPD_binary CV F1-macro was statistically indistinguishable (mode-fill 0.654 vs. MICE 0.647, within each other's noise). Decision: keep the simpler mode-fill.\par

\pard\f1\fs22\b\sa60 (c) Non-linear Age features and group-level importance\b0\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 Age\up6 2\up0\nosupersub  and clinical Age bands were tested as candidate features: neither was significantly associated with any target on its own (all p > 0.10), and adding them changed CV F1-macro negligibly for all three targets. However, group-level permutation importance (aggregating one-hot dummies back to their original survey question) revealed that standard per-dummy SHAP/RF-importance rankings had UNDER-STATED several raw variables for PHQ9 Result specifically: "Age of immediate older children" (grouped rank #2, absent entirely from the standard top-15) and "Disease before pregnancy" (grouped rank #3, also absent) -- a genuinely valuable methodological discovery, since it shows one-hot encoding can hide a variable's true importance by splitting its signal across several correlated dummy columns.\par
\page

\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 14. Clinical Literature Grounding\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 Each of the four composite domains was cross-checked against independent, external clinical literature (not just this project's own internal statistics):\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa60 - Social Support: an umbrella review of 77 meta-analyses found poor social support (RR 3.57) among the strongest PPD risk factors; a Chinese-population meta-analysis found OR 2.57 for lack of social support.\par
- Abuse / Intimate Partner Violence: meta-analyses report up to a 5.46-fold increased PPD risk (POR 5.46, 95% CI 3.94-7.56) for IPV exposure, with physical/emotional/sexual sub-types each independently significant.\par
- Economic Stability: Bangladesh-specific studies (Dhaka urban slums, rural cross-sectional surveys) directly confirm low economic status and household-resource poverty as established PPD risk factors in exactly this population, with prevalence as high as 39.4% in the most deprived urban-slum cohort -- the strongest literature match of the four, since it is population-specific rather than a global average.\par
- Neonatal/Delivery Stress: cesarean delivery (planned or emergency) is associated with a >15% increased risk of postpartum psychiatric conditions versus vaginal birth, with childbirth trauma (higher blood loss, emergency cesarean) mediating part of this risk.\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 All four composite domains therefore have independent EXTERNAL support, not just internal statistical significance -- meaningfully strengthening the justification for this specific 4-domain composite design.\par
\page

\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 15. Deeper Explainability: Interactions, Clustering, and Error Analysis\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\b\sa60 (a) SHAP without vs. with composites, and SHAP-additive group importance\b0\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 Re-running SHAP on the raw-only feature set (no composites) on the identical split showed the top features are individual raw answers; once composites are added, they rise to the top -- for PHQ9 Result specifically, the Social Support Index becomes the single most important feature overall, overtaking every raw answer. A SHAP-additive group-importance view (summing a group's one-hot dummies' SHAP values, since SHAP values are additive by construction) mostly agreed with the earlier permutation-based grouping, with one instructive exception: "Age of immediate older children" ranked #2 by permutation importance for PHQ9 Result but only #14 by SHAP-additive importance -- resolved by the interaction analysis below.\par

\pard\f1\fs22\b\sa60 (b) SHAP interaction values\b0\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 Pairwise SHAP interaction values (restricted to each target's top-20 features for tractability) revealed that the single strongest interaction in ALL THREE targets involves two composite indices (or a composite plus the top raw flag): Mental-Health-Risk \u215  Social-Support for EPDS Result, Social-Support \u215  Angry-after-birth for PHQ9 Result, and Angry-after-birth \u215  Social-Support for PPD_binary. Economic-Stability \u215  Mental-Health-Risk was consistently a top-5 interaction across all three targets -- a robust, cross-target finding that financial hardship measurably amplifies mental-health risk's effect on the prediction. This analysis also resolved the "Age of immediate older children" puzzle from (a): its strongest interaction (with Social Support) ranked 9th of 190 pairs for PHQ9 Result, confirming its true importance is interaction-driven rather than a simple main effect -- exactly the mechanism permutation importance is sensitive to and plain SHAP-sum is not.\par

\pard\f1\fs22\b\sa60 (c) Unsupervised cluster analysis\b0\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 K-Means clustering on a compact, clinically interpretable 6-feature space (the 4 weighted composites + Age + pregnancy count) -- deliberately NOT the full 112-dimension one-hot space -- found K=3 natural groups by silhouette score, using no PPD label at all. The resulting PPD_binary prevalence differed dramatically across these unsupervised groups: 66.4% in the "low support / low economic stability / high mental-health risk" cluster versus 18.6% in the "high support / high economic stability / low mental-health risk" cluster (chi-square p = 3.73e-28) -- a 3.6-fold spread. Because the clustering step never saw a single PPD label, this is a genuinely independent confirmation that the composite-index risk story reflects real structure in the data, not something a supervised model was merely trained to exploit.\par

\pard\f1\fs22\b\sa60 (d) Error analysis\b0\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 Splitting the PPD_binary held-out test set into true/false positives/negatives (TN=75, TP=48, FN=22, FP=15) and comparing composite-index values between correct and incorrect predictions found that BOTH types of error are explained almost entirely by the same two composites: false negatives (missed real PPD cases) had unusually LOW Mental-Health-Risk-Index and HIGH Social-Support-Index scores (an "atypical presentation" subgroup who do not outwardly show the usual risk factors); false positives showed the mirror-image pattern. This is a well-characterized, specific limitation rather than a vague one -- valuable for an honest discussion section.\par
\page

\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 16. Closing-the-Loop Feature Test\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 A final experiment directly tested whether the interaction pairs (Section 15b) and cluster structure (Section 15c) discovered during explainability analysis could be turned into an actual model improvement, by adding them as new engineered features (3 interaction product-terms; a fold-safe K-Means cluster-ID feature) to each target's own current-best model, evaluated by 5-fold cross-validation. Result: for every target, every configuration's improvement over baseline was SMALLER than that target's own fold-to-fold noise (standard deviation) -- i.e., no configuration produced a real, adoptable improvement. For PHQ9 Result, adding the interaction terms consistently made performance slightly WORSE. This is a valuable, honest negative result: it confirms that Random Forest and other tree ensembles already learn these feature interactions internally through their own splits (the SHAP interaction values were measuring existing learned interactions, not missed ones), and that a K-Means cluster label is an information-REDUCING transformation of continuous scores that are already present as direct inputs -- so no further gain was available from this direction. Decision: no configuration adopted; each target's model, as documented in Section 17, remains unchanged.\par
\page

