\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 18. Key Novelties and Contributions\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120\b 1. Four engineered Composite Risk Indices\b0  -- a documented, empirically-validated, data-driven weighting scheme (not a manual fudge), each significant across all 3 targets and independently reproduced by 3 separate methods.\par
\b 2. A genuinely leakage-free pipeline\b0  -- an explicit, code-enforced drop of all EPDS/PHQ-9 item and score columns, producing realistic (not suspiciously perfect) accuracy, directly contrasting with a comparable reference paper that shows signs of leakage.\par
\b 3. A statistically rigorous ablation study\b0  -- nested, leakage-safe feature selection, paired significance testing, and a follow-up higher-power repeated-CV test that correctly distinguished "no effect" from "underpowered test."\par
\b 4. Five independent explainability angles\b0  on one consistent model -- global SHAP importance, an arm-comparison "before/after" view, SHAP-additive group importance, SHAP pairwise interaction values, and an independent unsupervised clustering confirmation -- plus a targeted error analysis of the model's specific blind spot.\par
\b 5. Ordinal-aware evaluation and modeling\b0  -- Quadratic Weighted Kappa alongside F1-macro, and a genuine, adopted change of modeling approach (regression-then-threshold) for the hardest target, PHQ9 Result.\par
\b 6. An explicit, honestly-reported interpretability-over-accuracy trade-off\b0  -- documenting that a Stacking Ensemble is technically more accurate for PPD_binary, while explaining precisely why a single Random Forest was kept as the project's explainable backbone.\par
\b 7. Transparent negative results\b0  -- LR-learned weights, SMOTE, MICE imputation, Age features, and the final closing-loop feature test were all tested and, where they did not help, honestly reported as rejected rather than omitted -- itself evidence of methodological rigor.\par
\page

\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 19. Limitations\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 - The "Abuse" survey question's real-world meaning needs verification against the original Bangla questionnaire wording, since its empirical direction (No answers associating with higher PPD) is counter-intuitive and consistently reproduced across three independent methods, suggesting either a genuine but unusual finding or a translation/coding nuance rather than a modeling artifact.\par
- With n = 800 and, for PHQ9 Result, 5 imbalanced classes, statistical power for some comparisons (e.g. the original 5-fold ablation significance test) is limited; this was mitigated but not eliminated by the repeated-CV re-test.\par
- The error analysis identified a specific, characterized blind spot: mothers whose survey answers do not outwardly match the "typical" risk profile (low apparent mental-health risk, decent apparent support) despite genuinely meeting clinical PPD criteria are the mothers the model is most likely to miss.\par
- All results are internal, cross-validated estimates on a single-country, single-dataset sample; external validation on an independent Bangladeshi (or other South Asian) cohort would be needed before any clinical deployment claim.\par
\page

\pard\f0\fs30\b\cf2 20. Conclusion\cf1\b0\fs22\par
\pard\f1\fs22\sa120 This project delivers a complete, leakage-free, and thoroughly validated machine learning framework for early PPD detection in Bangladesh. Its central contribution -- four clinically-grounded Composite Risk Indices -- is supported by internal statistical testing, an ablation study with significance testing, independent external literature, an independently-learned alternative weighting scheme, and an unsupervised clustering analysis that never used a PPD label at all. The chosen models (Random Forest for EPDS Result and PPD_binary; Linear-Regression-then-threshold for PHQ9 Result) were selected not simply for the highest raw accuracy number, but through an explicit, reasoned balance between predictive performance and explainability -- and every explainability finding was further stress-tested by asking whether it could be turned into an actual model improvement, honestly reporting that it could not, which is itself informative: the current models already capture what these composite indices have to offer.\par
